WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.401 --> 00:00:03.636
-Our first guest tonight
is a Tony and Emmy-winning actor

00:00:03.636 --> 00:00:05.037
who spent eight years hosting

00:00:05.037 --> 00:00:06.939
"The Late Late Show
with James Corden."

00:00:06.939 --> 00:00:09.475
His new interview show,
"This Life of Mine..."

00:00:09.475 --> 00:00:13.679
premieres February 1st
on Sirius XM Stars channel 109

00:00:13.679 --> 00:00:15.247
and the Sirius XM app.

00:00:15.247 --> 00:00:16.649
Please welcome back to the show

00:00:16.649 --> 00:00:18.785
our good friend
James Corden, everybody.

00:00:18.785 --> 00:00:20.486
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:20.486 --> 00:00:36.002 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:36.002 --> 00:00:38.071
Welcome back, James.

00:00:38.071 --> 00:00:40.306
-Hi. How are you?
-Welcome back to the States.

00:00:40.306 --> 00:00:42.075
Welcome back to
a talk-show studio.

00:00:42.075 --> 00:00:43.542
-I know.
-You got out of the life.

00:00:43.542 --> 00:00:45.277
-I did.
-You got out of the life.

00:00:45.277 --> 00:00:48.214
-I did. And you can, too!

00:00:48.214 --> 00:00:50.115
-I do -- I do --
-That's all I'm doing.

00:00:50.115 --> 00:00:51.550
I went to see Jimmy last night.

00:00:51.550 --> 00:00:53.520
I'm gonna go and see Stephen
in a minute.

00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:55.354
And I say, "Listen. You don't...

00:00:55.354 --> 00:00:57.990
I know they make you feel
like you've got to do this."

00:00:57.990 --> 00:00:59.559
-I gotta do it.
-You don't!

00:00:59.559 --> 00:01:01.727
-I gotta do it tomorrow,
and then we gotta do it
the next day!

00:01:01.727 --> 00:01:04.163
-Oh, that's -- that's the thing.
You realize.

00:01:04.163 --> 00:01:06.900
-How much more time do you have
in your day, James?

00:01:06.900 --> 00:01:08.868
-Oh. Oh. There's a -- Yeah.

00:01:08.868 --> 00:01:11.737
But that can have -- That's
a double-edged sword, you know.

00:01:11.737 --> 00:01:15.341
Then I've got time
to really contemplate

00:01:15.341 --> 00:01:18.411
and go to quite a dark place.
-When you're keeping busy...

00:01:18.411 --> 00:01:20.679
-I've not been doing anything,
in truth.

00:01:20.679 --> 00:01:22.649
I really --
I really decided to --

00:01:22.649 --> 00:01:24.717
We were so conscious,
my wife and I,

00:01:24.717 --> 00:01:26.452
that we were moving.

00:01:26.452 --> 00:01:28.521
You know, we have three kids.
They're 12, 9, and 6.

00:01:28.521 --> 00:01:32.591
And to take them -- to lift
them up from Los Angeles

00:01:32.591 --> 00:01:34.360
and be back in London --

00:01:34.360 --> 00:01:37.330
For me then to leave
would be --

00:01:37.330 --> 00:01:40.600
and to go off
and do anything else would be --

00:01:40.600 --> 00:01:43.602
would be, you know,
such a stupid decision.

00:01:43.602 --> 00:01:46.105
So all we've been doing --
all we've been doing

00:01:46.105 --> 00:01:48.674
is just really
just focusing on trying to --

00:01:48.674 --> 00:01:51.510
We knew we were going to
hit bumps along the way.

00:01:51.510 --> 00:01:53.246
That was inevitable.
but to try and make them

00:01:53.246 --> 00:01:55.415
as soft as we could for them,
really.

00:01:55.415 --> 00:01:56.916
That was the biggest thing.

00:01:56.916 --> 00:01:58.551
-You were well-known in the UK

00:01:58.551 --> 00:02:00.353
before you came over here
to do your show,

00:02:00.353 --> 00:02:02.521
and then very quickly
you became well-known here.

00:02:02.521 --> 00:02:05.091
When you went back to the UK,
had they been familiar

00:02:05.091 --> 00:02:07.093
with the work
you were doing over here?

00:02:07.093 --> 00:02:09.095
Did "The Late Late Show"
make a mark?

00:02:09.095 --> 00:02:11.664
-Well, they --
I suppose because, you know,

00:02:11.664 --> 00:02:15.668
we came up -- we had a couple of
clips or segments that would --

00:02:15.668 --> 00:02:18.871
that would do very well online
and stuff, so they knew that.

00:02:18.871 --> 00:02:21.440
But nobody --
Because back at home in Britain,

00:02:21.440 --> 00:02:23.476
no one does a talk show
every night of the week.

00:02:23.476 --> 00:02:24.877
-Yes.
-So, like, Graham Norton,

00:02:24.877 --> 00:02:27.079
which is the biggest talk show
in the country

00:02:27.079 --> 00:02:28.914
and was a huge influence on me

00:02:28.914 --> 00:02:31.150
and the way
that we built our show

00:02:31.150 --> 00:02:35.488
is on once a week for, I think,
30 weeks of the year.

00:02:35.488 --> 00:02:38.424
And that is exactly
how it should be done.

00:02:38.424 --> 00:02:41.227
-I mean...
I'm so jealous of him.

00:02:41.227 --> 00:02:43.863
-Nobody -- Nobody can comp--
Nobody at home would comprehend

00:02:43.863 --> 00:02:46.432
because I'd be like, "Well,
no, because I'm doing a show."

00:02:46.432 --> 00:02:48.434
And they were,
"You just did it yesterday."

00:02:48.434 --> 00:02:50.702
I go, "Mm-hmm. Yeah, no, no.
I know. I'm..."

00:02:50.702 --> 00:02:52.104
"What are you doing?"

00:02:52.104 --> 00:02:55.174
"It's essentially the same,
just with different people.

00:02:55.174 --> 00:02:57.677
That's it. It's a long
fever dream really."

00:02:57.677 --> 00:02:59.712
-Also, the other thing
I'm so jealous --

00:02:59.712 --> 00:03:01.447
Every time you see
Graham Norton,

00:03:01.447 --> 00:03:04.150
it's four of the most famous
people in the world.

00:03:04.150 --> 00:03:06.051
-Yes.
-He always -- His guests are --

00:03:06.051 --> 00:03:08.487
Any one of us
would kill ourselves

00:03:08.487 --> 00:03:10.222
to have all of his guests
on one show.

00:03:10.222 --> 00:03:12.759
-Because he's doing it once
a week for 30 weeks of the year.

00:03:12.759 --> 00:03:15.427
So it's like -- It's all of
those -- Yeah, there's like --

00:03:15.427 --> 00:03:17.730
Look, if you were doing this
once a week,

00:03:17.730 --> 00:03:19.632
I don't make the cut here.

00:03:19.632 --> 00:03:21.767
I'm not here. Not a chance!

00:03:21.767 --> 00:03:23.669
-Oh, absolutely not.
-Not a chance!

00:03:23.669 --> 00:03:26.572
I'd be waiting outside hoping

00:03:26.572 --> 00:03:30.209
that there's some awful bout
of gastric flu or something,

00:03:30.209 --> 00:03:32.645
and then I might
just get on down.

00:03:32.645 --> 00:03:34.881
-Hanks went down. Bad news.
Hanks went down.

00:03:34.881 --> 00:03:36.081
Good news. Corden's outside.

00:03:36.081 --> 00:03:37.650
-Correct.
-Yeah.

00:03:37.650 --> 00:03:40.519
-Bradley --
Bradley Cooper slipped.

00:03:40.519 --> 00:03:43.522
Don't worry. Bring in --
That's it.

00:03:43.522 --> 00:03:45.825
So, you know, at this moment,
I'm very grateful

00:03:45.825 --> 00:03:47.493
that you do it every day.
-Yeah.

00:03:47.493 --> 00:03:50.463
Well, thank you. I'm grateful.
Look. I'm grateful to have you.

00:03:50.463 --> 00:03:54.032
How about -- Obviously so
well-known in both places.

00:03:54.032 --> 00:03:56.001
What is the difference
between how you're approached

00:03:56.001 --> 00:03:58.237
by people in LA
versus how you're approached

00:03:58.237 --> 00:04:00.806
by people in London?
-Well, you don't -

00:04:00.806 --> 00:04:03.709
You don't really get approached
by anyone in LA.

00:04:03.709 --> 00:04:06.445
Because, firstly, you're not
walking anywhere

00:04:06.445 --> 00:04:08.381
-Right.
-You're just in a car

00:04:08.381 --> 00:04:09.682
and then you're in a valet.

00:04:09.682 --> 00:04:12.618
But, also, even if
you're in a restaurant,

00:04:12.618 --> 00:04:15.888
you know, you're aware --
If you live in Los Angeles

00:04:15.888 --> 00:04:20.492
and you host a show at 12:35
in the morning on CBS,

00:04:20.492 --> 00:04:22.561
you know where you are
in the food chain.

00:04:22.561 --> 00:04:24.297
-Yeah.
-You know, like,

00:04:24.297 --> 00:04:26.266
Oprah was in here yesterday.

00:04:26.266 --> 00:04:27.933
They actually look at you
with disdain.

00:04:27.933 --> 00:04:32.071
You know what I mean? Like,
"Oh, it's you. Oh, my God."

00:04:32.071 --> 00:04:33.439
Like that.

00:04:33.439 --> 00:04:37.810
But in the UK, I, um --
I wrote a TV show

00:04:37.810 --> 00:04:39.945
that not many people
would ever know.

00:04:39.945 --> 00:04:41.680
I wrote a TV show,
"Gavin &amp; Stacey," which --

00:04:41.680 --> 00:04:43.649
[ Cheers and applause ]
Ohh.

00:04:43.649 --> 00:04:44.850
-There you go.

00:04:44.850 --> 00:04:47.487
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:04:47.487 --> 00:04:50.390
-I'm watching right now.
Right now you're like,

00:04:50.390 --> 00:04:52.792
"I should be getting residuals!"

00:04:52.792 --> 00:04:54.727
-Hang on. Are you --
Are you British?

00:04:54.727 --> 00:04:56.795
[ Cheers and applause ]
Are you? Are you?

00:04:56.795 --> 00:04:58.598
Whereabouts are you from?
-Kent!

00:04:58.598 --> 00:05:03.302
-Kent? Look out, guys.
Be careful. That...

00:05:03.302 --> 00:05:06.506
Whereabouts in Kent?
-Chatham.

00:05:06.506 --> 00:05:09.308
-Oh, they're thieves.
They are thieves.

00:05:09.308 --> 00:05:11.811
Aw! I love that you're here!
-Also, don't come here

00:05:11.811 --> 00:05:15.547
and now start the expectation
that I'm gonna do crowd work.

00:05:15.547 --> 00:05:17.516
We'll have people showing up.
They're going to go crazy

00:05:17.516 --> 00:05:19.018
every time they hear
where they're from.

00:05:19.018 --> 00:05:22.455
-No. So I did this show
called "Gavin &amp; Stacey,"

00:05:22.455 --> 00:05:25.358
which, to everybody's
surprise -- mostly me and my --

00:05:25.358 --> 00:05:28.627
Me and my best friend Ruth
Jones, We wrote it together.

00:05:28.627 --> 00:05:31.463
And so, really, more often
than anything else,

00:05:31.463 --> 00:05:32.965
people will come up to me

00:05:32.965 --> 00:05:36.835
and they'll say catchphrases
from that show.

00:05:36.835 --> 00:05:39.138
Can you remember a catchphrase?
-"What's occurrin'?"

00:05:39.138 --> 00:05:40.906
-"What's occurrin'?"
There you go.

00:05:40.906 --> 00:05:43.175
That's what people
will say to me all the time.

00:05:43.175 --> 00:05:46.311
Or more often than not, they'll
come up to me and they'll ask --

00:05:46.311 --> 00:05:48.480
Because the last episode
ended on a proposal.

00:05:48.480 --> 00:05:51.550
And they will ask,
"Did Smithy say yes or no?"

00:05:51.550 --> 00:05:53.152
Which I will never share.

00:05:53.152 --> 00:05:55.421
-What is the -- What is the
catchphrase? "What's the..."

00:05:55.421 --> 00:05:57.689
-No, it's --
Well, the show is set in, uh --

00:05:57.689 --> 00:05:59.926
Um, Sirius XM
are going to be so pleased

00:05:59.926 --> 00:06:01.660
that we're talking about --

00:06:01.660 --> 00:06:07.199
The show is set in a small town
out just outside of London,

00:06:07.199 --> 00:06:10.102
in Essex called Billericay, and
an island called Barry Island,

00:06:10.102 --> 00:06:12.338
which is in Wales,
just outside of Cardiff.

00:06:12.338 --> 00:06:14.406
And in Barry, they've got
a very particular accent.

00:06:14.406 --> 00:06:15.742
It's sort of like that. Right?

00:06:15.742 --> 00:06:18.077
And you say,
"Oh, what's occurrin'?"

00:06:18.077 --> 00:06:20.446
-Oh. "What's occurring?"
I got you. Got it.

00:06:20.446 --> 00:06:22.448
-She'd say,
"Oh, what's occurrin'?"

00:06:22.448 --> 00:06:24.116
And that's where that came from.
-Alright.

00:06:24.116 --> 00:06:26.352
-Yeah.
-So, uh, maybe watch that

00:06:26.352 --> 00:06:28.221
instead of the XM show?
What do you think?

00:06:28.221 --> 00:06:30.322
[ Laughter ]

00:06:30.322 --> 00:06:32.625
-I mean, look.
I get -- I get --

00:06:32.625 --> 00:06:35.394
I get paid for the attempt, man.

00:06:35.394 --> 00:06:37.530
-I have a lot. We're gonna --
The next segment

00:06:37.530 --> 00:06:41.234
is gonna be all Sirius XM.
We'll be right back with James.

